    "premiere": {
        "name": "Premiere Pro",
        "exe": PREMIERE_SUBPATH,
        "dxvk_conf": DXVK_CONF_SUBPATH,
        "overrides": WINEDLLOVERRIDES,           # "ucrtbase=n,b;dwrite=b"
        # ⭐ 2026-08-03 — GPU-ACCELERATED UI MODE, PRODUCTIONISED (was ~/premiere-gpu-mode.sh).
        # The prefix has MZ.Prefs.UseGpuAcceleration=true, and GPU mode WITHOUT this env blacks
        # the ENTIRE interface -- so a plain `neutron launch premiere` rendered black all morning
        # while we hunted a regression that did not exist. Premiere's UI drawbot is the D2D/GPU
        # one; these six are what make its output reach the screen:
        #   NEUTRON_D2D_DCDIRECT   -- D2D HwndRenderTargets render into the window's GDI paint
        #       surface instead of a DXGI swapchain nothing presents ("swapchain-stomp
        #       invisibility", d2d1_private.h:75). The single most load-bearing one.
        #   NEUTRON_GDI_PRESENT    -- DXVK presents non-FLIP swapchains through GDI so they
        #       compose with that same surface. Lives in the prefix's DXVK d3d11, not in wine.
        #   NEUTRON_DCOMP_NATIVE   -- same-process D2D-UI composition path (staged dcomp).
        #   NEUTRON_CEF_MAX_FPS    -- caps the CEF Home-screen repaint rate.
        #   NEUTRON_D2D_COMPOSEREAD -- full-client GetDC+BitBlt compose-read. ⚠️ Premiere WANTS
        #       this; AE must NOT have it (it paints a white screen over AE's canvas before a
        #       composition exists). This is exactly why env is per-profile.
        # NEUTRON_D2D_CLIPCHILDREN is deliberately absent: it defaults ON and only accepts "0",
        # so passing "1" was always inert.
        #
        # ⚠️ Three DLLs must also be staged in the runtime (dcomp.dll, windows.ui.dll,
        # winewayland.drv, all built from the dev tree 2026-08-03 and backed up as
        # *.pre-gpumode-prod). They are LEAKY EXPORTS -- no .mypatch reproduces them, so a fresh
        # `runtime install` will NOT have them and Premiere will be black again. Capturing them
        # as patches is the outstanding work. All three are env-gated OFF by default, which is
        # why staging them is safe for Photoshop/Lightroom/AE sharing this runtime.
        #
        # ⭐ 2026-08-08 — THREE MORE GATES MOVED IN FROM ~/premiere-soak.sh. Premiere was only
        # ever validated through that script, which sets these on top of the profile; every
        # `neutron launch premiere` (and therefore every Collider launch) ran with a strictly
        # smaller feature set than any session judged "working". Defaults in the soak script are
        # RTG=1, DIRG=1, so these values ARE the validated configuration:
        #   NEUTRON_WIC_READTHROUGH -- WIC bitmaps handed to D2D as GPU memory directly.
        #   NEUTRON_DCOMP_DIRECT    -- direct publish of the CEP/Captioneer panel surface.
        #   NEUTRON_HEAP_LARGE_CACHE-- large-block heap cache.
        # ⚠️ Keep this list and premiere-soak.sh's env block in sync; if they diverge again the
        # CLI and the validated path stop testing the same thing. (3rd occurrence of "check how
        # the app was previously validated" -- see INCIDENTS.md.)
        "nvapi": False,
        # ⭐ 2026-08-19 — NEUTRON_WHEEL_NOTCH: snap a high-resolution mouse wheel to whole
        # notches. A hi-res wheel (HID++ multiplier 8) delivers one physical detent as an
        # IRREGULAR burst of value120 fragments (measured: 15,15,60,30,75...), because the
        # layers below Wine coalesce by timing rather than by detent. Premiere's timeline
        # treats each WM_MOUSEWHEEL as one discrete step, so the same click scrubbed 3-8
